															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/peppersghost" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1553042); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1553042&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973930" alt="PeppersGhost" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1553042)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/peppersghost" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1553042); return false;" >PeppersGhost</a></span> <span class="odate time_1413880256 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">21 Oct 2014 08:30</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2136166">
									

<p>Thanks! This tale is heavily influenced by my own personal view of the Foundation, and when I published it I was fully prepared for the possibility that people would hate it due to conflicts with their own headcanons, so I deeply appreciate that you upvoted it on its own merits.</p>
<p>For what it's worth, I'm still on the fence about Humes myself. Since this story is basically a representation of my own headcanon, I tried to obliquely imply that the bit about Kant Counters being &quot;indeed real&quot; might be one of the altered facts alluded to in the opening disclaimer. Think about it this way: the guy who wrote this makes a very clear distinction between what he does and does not know, and yet he explicitly proclaims Kant Counters to be real despite the fact he's never seen one.</p>
<p>It's open to interpretation, but I like to think the Foundation added that part in to help hide the truth that Kant Counters– as well as Humes in general –don't actually exist. I think it'd be interesting if the whole thing were just a big myth perpetuated by the Foundation to falsely reassure its employees that the universe is still a fundamentally reasonable and explainable place.</p>

							</div>

								
					
