														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2136494">
									

<ul>
<li>Yup. They lose their memories once, barring some sort of incident that necessitates another dosage of amnestics, and even then it probably wouldn't be another full wipe. Erasing valuable years of job experience would adversely affect his performance.</li>
<li>I've always assumed that amnestics in general only affect episodic memory. A lot of SCP articles use them a way that suggests they can selectively erase memories of anomalous phenomena, so it seems natural that they would also be able to erase memories of people and life events.</li>
<li>There are special teams for teams for special situations and environments. This guy is just part of a regular team that handles the more run-of-the-mill anomaly retrievals. Any sort of underwater assignment would require a lot of prior training, so there are probably groups of D-Class and MTFs set aside for that purpose. When the location itself is either anomalous by its very nature or encompasses a broad variety of anomalous phenomena– such as alternate universes and &quot;haunted&quot; environments, respectively –the Foundation uses an entirely different set of preliminary containment procedures than what they use in general retrieval situations.</li>
</ul>

							</div>

								
					
