					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/peppersghost" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1553042); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1553042&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973930" alt="PeppersGhost" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1553042)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/peppersghost" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1553042); return false;" >PeppersGhost</a></span> <span class="odate time_1413908657 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">21 Oct 2014 16:24</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2136505">
									

<p>At the end of the day, the ethical and philosophical implications of Class-Omega amnestics are not the focus of this article, and exploring those concepts in a way that does them justice would require an entirely separate tale longer than this one.</p>
<p>In the Foundation universe, I think that it's probably a controversial issue. I imagine there are more than a few people on the ethics committee who voted against retaining Omega-dosed individuals as Class-Ds, but were outvoted by those who either thought that their crimes still warranted punishment or believed their usefulness in Foundation activities outweighed their potential usefulness to society. That doesn't necessarily make it right, but the Foundation has a history of doing what is practical, not what's nice.</p>

							</div>

								
