					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/andarielhalo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1750255); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1750255&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973930" alt="AndarielHalo" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1750255)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/andarielhalo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1750255); return false;" >AndarielHalo</a></span> <span class="odate time_1413913389 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">21 Oct 2014 17:43</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2136562">
									

<p>That sounds like something that would be imminently more important and interesting to cover, even in a style of released memos/minutes/notes. And honestly, if I were actually there and a part of that group, that's the sort of decision I would publicly resign over. It would be greatto read</p>
<p>And to lunge on VAElynx; the crime DOESN'T go away, but the purpose of punishment for a crime is indelibly rehabilitation and/or to prevent such a person from repeating the actions leading to the crime or the crime itself. Erasing someone's memory of their entire life is basically the Deus Ex Machina of rehabilitation. After that point, who are you &quot;punishing&quot; for the crime? The ex-criminal's body? Their organs? Their face for looking like the criminal?</p>
<p>Continuing to punish the &quot;husk&quot; of a criminal only really serves as revenge for the victim IF the victim either isn't aware that the person who hurt them is essentially dead, or if they simply don't care. That's not what justice/rehabilitation is about at all; that's essentially &quot;feel-good&quot; torture.</p>

							</div>

								
