														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2137272">
									

<p>That's not a relevant comparison because getting drunk isn't a crime in itself; it's attempting to handle dangerous heavy machinery while drunk which is the crime, and that doesn't require intent to harm.</p>
<p>Even leaving that aside, alcoholism is an addiction, so obviously a drunk will continue to get drunk, that's not the same as being the &quot;same old criminal/person thing here&quot; who will do it again. It is still their responsibility not to drive or handle dangerous things while drunk. If they genuinely cannot control themselves while drunk, then they must take pre-emptive measures to ensure they don't do so, such as having a designated driver, or having the bartender call them a cab, or to just stay home and drink there.</p>
<p>As well, the punishments for first time drunk driving offenders are decidedly not punitive in nature, depending on whether or not anyone was injured; first timers receive generally rehabilitation-based treatment and restrictions.</p>
<p>Regardless, the fact is that the motivations of the many people convicted of violent crimes are a vast malaise of psychology vs environment, etcetera, the majority of which could see a significant change in behavior if they had people who cared about them and/or were responsible for them and made the effort to reform them. Even genuine violent sociopaths could be rehabilitated in this manner</p>

							</div>

								
					
